{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document: API Client Unit Tests\n\n## Problem Statement\nThe internal ops dashboard depends on `src/api/client.js` for API interactions, but there is currently no automated test coverage verifying its behavior. Without unit tests, changes to API handling risk undetected regressions, broken error states, or uncaught payload parsing failures.\n\n## Goals\n- Establish comprehensive unit test coverage strictly for the API client module in `src/api/client.js`.\n- Mock all HTTP network requests so tests run reliably, fast, and independently of external test servers.\n- Verify error handling for common API failure modes (e.g., HTTP errors, malformed responses).\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Testing React components such as `src/App.jsx` or `src/components/Header.jsx`.\n- End-to-end (E2E) or integration testing against live or staging backend servers.\n\n## User Impact\nOps team members using the dashboard will benefit from reduced application instability and regression-free releases, ensuring critical operational dashboards remain dependable.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- Are there specific business-critical status codes or API response contracts (such as custom error payload structures) that need explicit acceptance verification in this test suite?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document: API Client Unit Tests\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Test Harness Setup**: `package.json` currently contains only production dependencies (`react`, `react-router-dom`) and lacks any test runner or test scripts. A test runner (such as Vitest or Jest) must be installed under `devDependencies` and configured with a `\"test\"` execution script.\n- **Mocking Strategy**: As confirmed in Q&A, network calls will not hit a test server and must be mocked. An HTTP mocking approach (e.g., standard runner mocks for `globalThis.fetch` or Mock Service Worker) must be established.\n- **Target Module**: All test cases will target functions exported by `src/api/client.js` (e.g., `src/api/client.test.js`).\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- `package.json`: Missing test runner and mocking utilities; these need to be introduced as new dependencies.\n- `src/api/client.js`: Tests must explicitly cover:\n  - Successful HTTP responses (2xx) and JSON parsing.\n  - Client and server error responses (HTTP 4xx and 5xx).\n  - Network failures, abort signals, and request timeouts.\n  - Malformed or non-JSON response payloads.\n- **Runtime Environment**: Ensure global `fetch` is supported and polyfilled if necessary within the chosen test runner's Node.js runtime.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- Which test framework should be selected (e.g., Vitest vs. Jest)? Vitest offers minimal configuration if modern bundling is used, whereas Jest is a common alternative.\n- Which mocking library or pattern should be standardized across the repository (e.g., native `fetch` spying via runner mocks vs. MSW for network-level interception)?\n\n## Estimate\n- 2–4 days (up to 7 days worst case, accounting for initial framework bootstrapping, configuration, and comprehensive edge-case mocking)."}